sentences.text:
The sum and substance of arguments of Ld. Counsel revisionist are as under:-
(i) That the impugned order is bad in law, resulting in grave miscarriage of justice;
(ii) That Ld Trial court has failed to appreciate that in the FIR No. 422/2001 under revision, there are no allegations against the revisionist in particular except the bald allegations some officials of MCD were involved and facilitated the accused persons;
(iii) That Ld. Trial Court failed to appreaciate that the limited role of revisionist is that he was Zonal Inspector In-charge at the time when mutation was allowed and he had merely submitted report to A & C Deptt., MCD and calculated tax;
(iv) That Ld Trial court failed to consider that the revisionist cannot be held liable for fraud/forgery, if any, committed by accused persons who themselves gave undertaking in form of indemnity bond to MCD, if the title is found defective;
(v) That Ld Trial Court failed to appreciate that Ld. ASJ vide order dated 08.08.2003 while granting bail to the revisionist opined that no offence is made out against revisionist and if mutation is wrongly granted, MCD is open to cancel it;
(vi) That the Ld. Trial Court failed to appreciate that the respondent no. 2 did not challenge the Will, possession of other accused persons and order dated 24.09.1998 of MCD Tribunal which became final, whereby appeal was allowed and it was held that there appears no defect in title of other accused persons and defect, if any, found can be rectified by MCD by revoking building plans;
(vii) That Ld. Trial Court failed to appreciate that application for mutation was submitted the accused persons on 21.04.1994 i.e. prior to the joining of the revisionist in A & C Deptt, MCD on Crl Rev. No. 149/2021 Om Dutt Sharma Vs State (Govt of NCT Of Delhi) Page No

4A. Ld. Counsel for revisionist has relied upon following judgments in support of his contentions :-
a) R.R.Gautam and Anr. vs. State and Anr. 2008(1) JCC 766;
b) Union of India vs. Prafulla Kumar Samal & Anr. AIR 1979 SC 366;
c) State of Karnatka s. L. Muniswamy, AIR 1977 SC 1489;
d) State of Bihar vs. Ramesh Singh, AIR 1977 SC 2018;
e) State of M.P. s. S.B. Johari, 2000 Crl. L.J 944 (SC);
f) P.Vijayan vs. State of Kerala 2010 Crl. L.J. 1427;
5. Per contra, Ld. Addl PP for State/ respondent vehemently argued that there is no infirmity in the impugned order as the same was passed after considering all the material available on record and keeping in view the facts and circumstances of the present case.

